        {
            "type": "section",
            "positioning": {
                "marginTop": 20
            },
            "elements": [
                {
                    "type": "header",
                    "content": "The Electronics",
                    "id": "electronics",
                    "level": 2
                },
                {
                    "type": "header",
                    "level": 3,
                    "size": 2,
                    "content": "Basic Design"
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 5
                    },
                    "content": "The electronics involve reading the plant signal and sending them to be processed by the backend. Initially, I was given force-sensitive resistors to test with. Because they didn't need amplification, I used an Arduino's built in ADC. The Arduino then sends the formatted data to the sender ESP32 over UART. There are two ESP32s that communicate over an ESP-NOW network to send the data wirelessly."
                },
                {
                    "type": "image",
                    "url": "https://assets.joshtreid.com/images/pdigtwinterface/electronics-no-plant.webp",
                    "alt": "Picture of electronics with breadboard, Arduino, ESP32, and force-sensitive resistors.",
                    "title": "Electronics - No Plant Sensor Test",
                    "screenSizes": {
                        "small": 90,
                        "medium": 70,
                        "large": 50
                    },
                    "width": 70,
                    "y": 50,
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 10
                    }
                },
                {
                    "type": "header",
                    "level": 3,
                    "size": 2,
                    "content": "Amplifier Circuit"
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 5
                    },
                    "content": "After that circuit worked well enough, I was given a plant to test real stimuli. An issue I ran into was that all my readings were 0V. The Arduino couldn't read the tiny voltages and the high impedance plant tissue. To fix this, I used a combination of an INA333 and ADS1115. The INA333 is a high impedance input that can amplify the small voltages into usable voltages. Then, the ADS1115 acts as the Arduino's ADC with a higher resolution. Instead of using the Arduino's analog pins, I just connected the I2C pins to the ADS1115."
                },
